## Answer Key & Rationale

**Q1 — C.** A cascade with a verification signal keeps small-model economics on the easy
majority and catches misroutes, which here are detectable because answers must be cited. (A:
would be right at trivial volume, but it pays top-tier rates on 85% of traffic for no
measured gain. B: accepts confident wrong answers even though you have a cheap way to catch
them. D: a guess — the evals say only the largest model passes the hard queries.)

**Q2 — A.** Configure reasoning depth per workload. The memo gain was measured, and the
autocomplete budget can’t absorb thinking latency. Uniform configuration is a convenience,
not a requirement. (C: prompt-level reasoning is a real technique, but it wasn’t the
evaluated option, so it swaps a measured 17-point gain for a guess. B: still adds latency to
autocomplete, and a small budget may erase the memo gain. D: streaming helps perceived
latency for long outputs, but it can’t make a 300 ms field suggestion wait out a thinking
phase.)

**Q3 — D.** Error analysis puts the loss at the image-to-text conversion. The fix is to stop
discarding the modality, with the cost trade-off measured rather than assumed. (A and B:
improve the lossy step instead of removing it, and a caption can’t anticipate every detail
severity depends on. C: more reasoning can’t recover information that was never in the
input.)

**Q4 — C, E.** Choose the model per step. The high-volume step where the small model is at
parity carries almost all the cost, and the low-volume reasoning step carries almost all the
value. (A: ignores the measured quality drop on step 2. D: a plausible compensation, but it
contradicts the evals and saves little, since step 2 is 0.1% of the volume. B: consistency
is not a requirement when the steps are different tasks.)

**Q5 — B.** Templates that inject untrusted variables need a structural boundary:
instructions in one place, clearly delimited data in another. (A: filters by surface form,
so it breaks legitimate reviews and paraphrased attacks slip through. C: the inverted trap —
it raises untrusted text to system-level authority. D: a louder instruction with still no
boundary between instruction and data.)

**Q6 — C.** Separate content by stability and authority. The stable policy belongs in the
system prompt, where it holds authority and forms a cacheable prefix; per-request retrieved
content goes in the user turn as delimited data. (B: a positional trick that leaves the
documents at system authority and puts varying content first, so nothing caches. A: drops
the policy’s authority entirely. D: multiplies cost and loses cross-document reasoning.)

**Q7 — A.** A “never” requirement can’t rest on a probabilistic control alone. The prompt
reduces attempts, and a deterministic output check enforces the rule. (B: good prompt
hygiene that lowers the rate but can’t reach “never” against novel framings. C: the model
has dosage knowledge from training, not just from retrieval. D: consistent on identical
input, but each new role-play is a different input.)

**Q8 — D.** This is the inverted failure, over-refusal, caused by an underspecified
boundary. The fix is an operational scope definition, not a looser or louder one. (A:
overcorrects and drops the exclusions product still requires. B: two vague instructions
pulling against each other. C: softens how refusals feel without reducing wrong refusals.)

**Q9 — B.** Cache matching is on the exact prefix, and tools come before the system prompt
in it. An unchanged system prompt doesn’t help if what precedes it varies. Unstable ordering
makes even identical entitlement sets miss. (A: traffic volume didn’t fall, and expiry
wouldn’t take a 90% hit rate to near zero. C: longer static content doesn’t stop caching. D:
caching works with tools, and tool definitions are themselves cacheable.)

**Q10 — A.** Skills use progressive disclosure: metadata is always present, bodies load on
demand. So the design risk is ambiguous descriptions, not the count. (B: consolidation makes
every loaded body larger and blurs the descriptions, which hurts triggering. C: the model
selects Skills from their descriptions without being named, and the metadata does occupy
context. D: conflates two mechanisms — caching discounts a repeated prefix, it doesn’t make
content free or remove it from context.)

**Q11 — C.** Modular prompts separate what must be shared from what varies, so each team
changes only its own module and shared rules have a single source. (A: removes the coupling
but duplicates the shared rules 30 times, and they will drift. B: a process patch on a
structural coupling. D: trades a fast, editable, reviewable artefact for slow retraining
cycles.)

**Q12 — A, D.** A stable prefix with a breakpoint makes the contract reusable, and a
lifetime longer than the typical pause keeps it warm between questions. (E: false premise —
caching applies to any repeated prefix, including one user’s own repeated requests. B: puts
varying content first and destroys the prefix. C: loses the clause-level detail Q&A depends
on, and costs a summary call every turn.)

**Q13 — D.** Pick the metric from the cost of each error. At low prevalence with roughly
650:1 cost asymmetry, recall on the rare class dominates. (B: F1 is the standard answer to
imbalance, but it weights both error types equally when their costs differ by orders of
magnitude. C: optimises the $60 error at the expense of the $40,000 one. A: the headline
number is beaten by doing nothing.)

**Q14 — B.** A security metric has to measure whether attacks succeed, on a repeatable set,
every release. (A: a monitoring signal that counts what was caught and is blind to what got
through. C: valuable, but a point-in-time check with no regression coverage as prompts and
tools change. D: measures refusals on benign inputs, which says nothing about adversarial
ones.)

**Q15 — C.** A one-sided safety metric rewards refusing everything. Over-refusal has to be
measured directly, on its own test set. (D: moves a dial without measuring which answers
become allowed, possibly harmful ones. B: a lagging, unspecific signal that doesn’t say
which requests were wrongly refused. A: optimises the measured side and ignores the harm on
the other.)

**Q16 — B, C.** The team optimised a proxy that has diverged from the goal. Replace it with
measures of the qualities users value and of the task the summary exists to serve. (D: swaps
one surface-overlap proxy for another. E: overcorrects into slow, coarse feedback. A:
optimises the proxy harder.)

**Q17 — A.** When two variables change together, the result belongs to the combination.
Attributing it to one of them needs a design that separates them. (C: more traffic makes the
combined effect more certain, not attributable. D: assumes exactly the attribution the test
couldn’t make. B: correct for adopting the whole variant here, wrong for extrapolating one
component elsewhere.)

**Q18 — D.** At 400 per arm, a 3-point gap sits well inside the noise, and stopping at a
momentary significant peek inflates false positives. Predefine the sample size and honour
it. (A: the classic peeking error. B: counts noisy daily wins instead of estimating the
effect. C: switching metrics mid-test is its own validity problem, and nothing here suggests
the metric is wrong.)

**Q19 — B.** Guardrail metrics exist so that a primary-metric win can’t mask a regression in
a critical segment. A breach stops the ship until it is understood. (C: correct once the
cause is known and confined to that segment, but here the cause is unknown, and the same
behaviour may be quietly harming other segments. D: waiting while the regression runs on
live customers. A: treats guardrails as decoration.)

**Q20 — C.** Iterative improvement needs memory: every fixed failure becomes a permanent
test, so each change is checked against all past fixes, not just the current one. (A:
valuable for aggregate impact, but rare regressions don’t show up in aggregate metrics. D:
still checks only the current issue. B: stops improvement to avoid regression.)
